<!-- MODULE 5: OPENING NARRATIVE -->
<section class="cs-exec">
  <div class="cs-exec-inner">
    <p class="cs-exec-body">For an established DTC pet care brand, strong branded search volume is a sign the business is working. Pet owners who find a product their animal responds well to tend to reorder it — and search for the brand specifically when they do. That demand converts reliably. It is also not the same thing as acquiring new customers, and it should not be measured alongside them as though it were.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">A blended ROAS that combines branded and non-branded demand gives an accurate account-level picture while leaving the more important question unanswered: how much of that return comes from buyers already looking for the brand, and how much comes from reaching people who didn&rsquo;t know the brand yet? For an established DTC brand, that distinction matters for how the account is managed and what the results actually represent.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">The account was already producing a strong blended result. The challenge was understanding what that result represented.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">Branded and non-branded demand were not separated clearly enough to evaluate independently. Searches from people already looking for the brand were being measured alongside broader product and category demand, making it harder to see how each part of the account was contributing.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">This wasn't a sign of poor management. It was a sign the account had grown past a structure that made sense when it was smaller.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">We reorganized the account around clearer campaign responsibilities: branded demand, high-intent non-brand Search, broader product discovery through Shopping and Performance Max, and upper-funnel activity measured in the context of its role.</p>
    <p class="cs-exec-body" style="margin-top:18px;">During the documented January&ndash;May 2026 period, the eight campaigns generated $221K in attributed revenue from $30.9K in managed spend, a 7.16&times; blended ROAS. Within the restructured account, Brand Search recorded 24.92&times; ROAS, while non-brand and broader-demand campaigns could be evaluated separately.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 6: BRAND DEMAND INSIGHT -->
<section class="cs-challenge" aria-labelledby="brand-demand-heading">
  <div class="cs-challenge-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2" id="brand-demand-heading">The Brand Demand Was Already There. The New Structure Made It Easier to Measure.</h2>
    <p class="cs-para">Before the rebuild, branded and non-branded queries were not separated clearly enough to evaluate independently. High-converting branded demand contributed to the account-level result, while broader acquisition activity was harder to assess on its own terms.</p>
    <p class="cs-para">The rebuild gave branded demand a clearer campaign responsibility and separated higher-intent non-brand Search from broader product discovery. That did not create the client's brand equity. It made the different sources of performance easier to measure, manage, and discuss.</p>
    <p class="cs-para">Blended ROAS remains commercially useful. But by itself, it cannot explain how much performance comes from existing brand demand, broader category demand, product discovery, or upper-funnel investment.</p>

    <!-- MODULE 6a: ACCOUNT OBSERVATION — what blended ROAS could not show -->
    <aside class="pc-obs-module" aria-label="What the blended number could not show">
      <span class="pc-obs-eyebrow">What the Blended Number Could Not Show</span>
      <div class="pc-obs-list" role="list">
        <div class="pc-obs-item" role="listitem">
          <span class="pc-obs-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div>
            <div class="pc-obs-label">Brand Demand</div>
            <p class="pc-obs-body">People already searching for the brand were converting at a very different rate from broader product and category searches. A single blended figure could not separate these two sources.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-obs-item" role="listitem">
          <span class="pc-obs-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div>
            <div class="pc-obs-label">Acquisition Performance</div>
            <p class="pc-obs-body">Non-brand activity needed to be evaluated without branded conversions in the same measurement frame. Combined, the two were difficult to diagnose independently.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="pc-obs-item" role="listitem">
          <span class="pc-obs-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div>
            <div class="pc-obs-label">Upper-Funnel Investment</div>
            <p class="pc-obs-body">Discovery activity served a different stage of demand and required a broader measurement framework than immediate platform ROAS alone could provide.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </aside>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- MODULE 7: FINDINGS -->
<section class="cs-findings" aria-labelledby="findings-heading">
  <div class="cs-findings-inner">
    <h2 class="cs-h2" id="findings-heading">What the Account Review Revealed</h2>

    <div class="cs-findings-list" role="list">
      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-finding-title">Branded and Non-Branded Demand Were Not Clearly Separated</div>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Brand-name searches and broader product or category searches were not separated clearly enough to evaluate as distinct sources of demand. Because these query types can convert at very different rates, combining them made the blended result less useful for diagnosing acquisition performance independently.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-finding-title">Non-Brand Campaigns Covered Different Intent Levels</div>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">Higher-intent product searches and broader category discovery were being evaluated within a structure that did not make their different conversion behavior easy to interpret. Separating campaign responsibilities so higher-intent Search could be assessed independently from broader product discovery made the performance of both easier to evaluate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="cs-finding" role="listitem">
        <span class="cs-finding-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
        <div>
          <div class="cs-finding-title">Upper-Funnel Activity Needed a Different Measurement Framework</div>
          <p class="cs-finding-body">The account had limited dedicated upper-funnel activity. That meant Google Ads was primarily capturing demand closer to conversion rather than deliberately testing broader reach within the platform. We introduced a dedicated upper-funnel layer and evaluated it separately from Search and Shopping, not expected to match Brand Search on immediate platform ROAS.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- MODULE 8: DIAGNOSTIC TRANSITION -->
    <div class="cs-mid-hook">
      <p class="cs-mid-hook-text">If branded and non-branded demand are not separated clearly, blended ROAS can make acquisition performance harder to interpret. The Growth Diagnostic examines where account-level performance is coming from, and whether the campaign structure makes those differences visible.</p>
      <a href="../growth-diagnostic-landing.html#book" class="cs-mid-hook-link">See What the Diagnostic Covers &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

